    "stem": "Wasim starts at $9$ and applies the Collatz rule: if the current number is even, take half of it; if it is odd, multiply by $3$ and add $1$. He writes $9$, $28$, $14$, $7$, $22$, $11$, $34$, $17$, $52$, $26$, $13$, $40$, $20$, $10$, $5$, $16$, $8$, $4$, $2$, $1$.\n\nWhich claim is correct?",
    "item_type": "mcq",
    "options": {
      "A": "After $7$ the next term is $21$, using only the multiply-by-$3$ step.",
      "B": "Reaching $1$ from $9$ proves that every whole-number start reaches $1$.",
      "C": "The chain is one Collatz example that reaches $1$; it does not prove the rule for every start.",
      "D": "The Collatz rule applies only to even starts, so the odd terms $9$ and $7$ are mistakes."
    },
